{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-333274" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=6x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=6y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074380/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd0b352ba4ab53d8524345302164581ff330694ccf4e2fda7b18de38c0e71fcf"} {"question" "q-333274" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=6$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{6t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{6t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={e}^{6t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNotice that for each vector $[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n${e}^{6t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince ${e}^{6t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074380/assets/404c1f68f360e2916a6281033c8a7e9771b648c61156da903ce2f66b931b924e.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074380/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd0b352ba4ab53d8524345302164581ff330694ccf4e2fda7b18de38c0e71fcf"} {"question" "q-333274" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074380/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "15fb5b5e98c6f1c030907150dcd93061f7675eed3f66df92dd6ff239cefe73ba"} {"question" "q-333274" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074380/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd0b352ba4ab53d8524345302164581ff330694ccf4e2fda7b18de38c0e71fcf"} {"question" "q-333274" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074380/assets/404c1f68f360e2916a6281033c8a7e9771b648c61156da903ce2f66b931b924e.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074380/assets/54eba0116ac434bdadbc878e8bd173b6174a42642cfcfe6825a8bdb178a3282c.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074380/assets/7e48266d30ae7c204b25e6eddca968d1b4ce9cd8e1c7b19cd20247fa66070f0b.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074380/assets/eba955015a4b919c65dc24facb0baa6e745df134feac32feeae07c99d40ff4ec.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074380/assets/ff337e0120343c7c956afa5e49ea3b482fbd79fe363783a018842d1bfeb340d6.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074380/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd0b352ba4ab53d8524345302164581ff330694ccf4e2fda7b18de38c0e71fcf"} {"question" "q-333274" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074380/assets/404c1f68f360e2916a6281033c8a7e9771b648c61156da903ce2f66b931b924e.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074380/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd0b352ba4ab53d8524345302164581ff330694ccf4e2fda7b18de38c0e71fcf"} {"question" "q-333589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+5y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector $v=[3,\\text{ }3]^{T}$ corresponds to the repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of the system's matrix $A.$ The vector $w=[0,\\text{ }3]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{4t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{{field-1}}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{4t}{{field-2}}+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}3t \\\\ 3t+3\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{4t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074380/assets/6ec9eac46644188b8b41a1a7ea28ca0542ee7e5c69d713028481904e90dd557a.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}3t \\\\ 3t+3\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x={{field-6}} \\\\ y=3(t+1){e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.20$ | $0$ | {{field-7}} |\n| $y$ | $0.20$ | $3$ | {{field-8}} |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074380/assets/b0be26a1544bbb3117afab15d004bafee70d831fffab4b1f6921870871ac4768.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074380/assets/64e685f4ea06a8f497ddfbe50a092a0362b8c92a9d2f78f9da2a8164113a785a.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074380/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd0b352ba4ab53d8524345302164581ff330694ccf4e2fda7b18de38c0e71fcf"} {"question" "q-333589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =4,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{4t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}3t \\\\ 3t+3\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074380/assets/6ec9eac46644188b8b41a1a7ea28ca0542ee7e5c69d713028481904e90dd557a.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}3t \\\\ 3t+3\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3t{e}^{4t} \\\\ y=3(t+1){e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.20$ | $0$ | $0.45$ |\n| $y$ | $0.20$ | $3$ | $4.92$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074380/assets/b0be26a1544bbb3117afab15d004bafee70d831fffab4b1f6921870871ac4768.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074380/assets/64e685f4ea06a8f497ddfbe50a092a0362b8c92a9d2f78f9da2a8164113a785a.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074380/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd0b352ba4ab53d8524345302164581ff330694ccf4e2fda7b18de38c0e71fcf"} {"question" "q-333589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074380/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "15fb5b5e98c6f1c030907150dcd93061f7675eed3f66df92dd6ff239cefe73ba"} {"question" "q-333589" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074380/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd0b352ba4ab53d8524345302164581ff330694ccf4e2fda7b18de38c0e71fcf"} {"question" "q-333589" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "t{e}^{-4t}"] ["math" "t{e}^{4t}"] ["math" "{e}^{-4t}"] ["math" "{e}^{4t}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074380/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd0b352ba4ab53d8524345302164581ff330694ccf4e2fda7b18de38c0e71fcf"} {"question" "q-333589" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{4t}"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074380/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd0b352ba4ab53d8524345302164581ff330694ccf4e2fda7b18de38c0e71fcf"} {"question" "q-333589" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074380/state.json" "file_sha256" "dd0b352ba4ab53d8524345302164581ff330694ccf4e2fda7b18de38c